WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:13.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  since the dawn of time  we have raised our eyes to the sky  observing  and dreaming  of the unknown worlds that the universe  might shelter  imagine that we could leave earth  and travel through space and time  in order to explore these inaccessible  places  discovering the cosmos  [Music]  we are one and a half billion kilometers  away from the sun  seen from here our star is no more than  a dot  lost in an immensity  in front of us  stan saturn  [Music]  it is the sixth planet of the solar  system  [Music]  saturn is a huge ball of gas  ten times larger than earth the gas  giant's diameter measures nearly 120 000  kilometers  for a mass of 500 000 billion  billion tons  being so far from the sun  it takes almost 30 years to orbit around  it  [Music]  here  one year amounts to 11 000 days on earth  [Music]  the very thick atmosphere of saturn  is composed mainly of hydrogen  to which is added some helium  and traces of other rare gases  these gases which are very light provide  saturn with a remarkably low average  density  if it could be submerged in a large  enough ocean  it would float on the surface of the  water  [Music]  [Music]  covered in yellow and orange colors  sutton's atmosphere is far from uniform  its color is due to the chemical  elements that compose it  [Music]  it sometimes presents blue shades  resulting from complex phenomena related  to how light scatters  these are the same phenomena that give  our sky its blue colour  on earth  [Music]  on saturn's surface a freezing  temperature of negative 180 degrees  celsius prevails  gas clouds circulate around the planet  and form a stack of horizontal  [Music]  stripes in this incessant flow it  sometimes happens that huge  thunderstorms form  these extremely powerful electric storms  can last for several months and extend  over thousands of kilometers  giving rise to raging winds that can  reach incredible speeds  [Music]  [Music]  at the north pole  the course of the winds draws a very  strange cloud pattern  its mysterious hexagonal shape has yet  to find a definite explanation  in ultraviolet light  we can also distinguish magnificent  auroras  [Music]  these intriguing polar lights  similar to those found on earth arise  from the interaction of solar winds with  the planet's magnetic field  saturn is surrounded by an impressive  system of concentric rings  these rings are made up of fine  particles of ice and dust  which revolve around the planet  extremely thin  they are only a few dozens of meters  thick  while spanning hundreds of thousands of  kilometers in radius  the origin of these rings is still a  mystery  they are perhaps the remnants of a moon  which would have ventured too close to  the planet  torn and distorted by the attraction of  saturn it would have eventually exploded  pulverized by the planet's tidal forces  [Music]  saturn is outrageously massive  it's very strong gravitational influence  allows it to keep in orbit over 60 moons  some of these moons orbit inside the  rings  among them for instance  is pan  orbiting within the anchor gap  its gravitational pull deflects the  trajectories of surrounding particles  [Music]  the periodic passage of the satellite  forces this division to stay open  and separates the rings which are  situated on either side  this is prometheus  pandora  [Music]  prometheus and pandora are two shepherd  moons which work together  orbiting very close to each other the  two moons deflect the trajectories of  particles around them  and force them to be confined inside a  very narrow ring  barely a hundred kilometers wide  as we move away from saturn and its  rings  we encounter a wide variety of moons  [Music]  each of them with unique characteristics  240 000 kilometers from saturn  lies enceladus  smaller than our moon  enceladus is however one of saturn's  largest  satellites  this moon exhibits strange plumes  gazer-like formations which rise several  hundred kilometers above its surface  composed of particles of ice  gas  and dust  these jets of matter emerge from  cryovolcanoes scattered across the  surface of the satellite  they bear witness to an intense internal  activity  on its surface  enceladus is covered with ice  riddled with impact craters  and notched with cracks and volcanic  faults  this moon remains constantly in a frozen  state  at an average temperature of negative  200 degrees celsius  despite this very low surface  temperature  the geological activity of enceladus  betrays the existence of an ocean of  liquid water under the ice  as well as the presence of organic  compounds  [Music]  two ingredients essential  for the development of life  [Music]  we are now more than a million  kilometers away from saturn  it is at this distance that its largest  satellite orbits  titan  titan is home to a completely different  world from enceladus  surrounded by a very dense atmosphere  consisting mainly of nitrogen  we find here a very rich climate  with clouds  rains of methane  and even seasons  like on earth  this thick cloud layer hides a surface  with impressive variety  not entirely unlike some of the  landscapes found on earth  crossed by rivers and lakes of methane  titan is also speckled with volcanoes  mountains  and sand dunes  although frozen given its distance from  the sun  titan surprisingly recalls the first  moments of our planet  the chemical composition of its  atmosphere contains complex organic  molecules  which might remind us of the prebiotic  conditions  at the origin of life on earth  visible from earth  it has been observed since prehistoric  times  saturn has always fascinated astronomers  its gigantic size and majestic rings  make it a marvel of the solar system  better known today than ever before  it still holds many mysteries  which have yet  to be discovered  you

